<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hayashi Razan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1583">1583</a>–<a href="/years/1657">1657</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hayashi Razan was a Japanese Neo-Confucian scholar who served the first four Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> as adviser and drafter of documents. A follower of the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>, he promoted a philosophy of hierarchical social order and loyalty that suited the new regime. The private academy he founded in Edo in 1630 became, under his descendants, the official shogunal school, the Shōheikō.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hayashi-razan.jpg" alt="Hayashi Razan" width="320" height="328" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%9E%97%E7%BE%85%E5%B1%B1_%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hayashi Razan, also known as Hayashi Dōshun, built his life's work on early studies with Fujiwara Seika (1561–1619), the first Japanese scholar known for a close study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> and his commentators. Seika, a court noble who had become a Buddhist priest, turned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and gathered scholars around him whose thought was shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>. Their teaching treated the individual as a functionary in a society that settles naturally into a hierarchy of four classes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, farmers, artisans and merchants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1607 Razan was accepted as a political adviser to the second shōgun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Tokugawa Hidetada</a>, and he went on to serve as tutor and adviser to the first four Tokugawa shōguns. He held the honorific title Daigaku-no-kami, which became hereditary in his family, as did the headship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s Confucian academy, later known as the Yushima Seidō, which stood at the head of the country's system of education and training. Equating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> with the cultured governing class, he helped legitimise the military government in its first years and urged samurai to cultivate learning, summed up in his saying, no true learning without arms and no true arms without learning.</p><p>Razan blended Confucian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> practice, arguing that Shinto was a provisional and local form of Confucian ideas, which allowed a Confucian reading of shrine rituals. In a private essay, Jimmu Tennō Ron, he argued that Emperor Jimmu and the imperial line descended from an offshoot of a Chinese royal house through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-taibo">Wu Taibo</a>, a view considered too dangerous to publish widely. He is also credited with first listing the Three Views of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Razan died on 7 March 1657. His third son, Hayashi Gahō, succeeded him as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s chief scholar, finished works his father had begun and in 1670 published the 310 volumes of the Honchō-tsugan, a comprehensive history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>; Gahō and his brother Hayashi Dokkōsai edited Razan's collected writings. His ideas dominated the shogunate's ideology until the end of the 18th century, and some Japanese scholars have called him the founder of modern historical research in Japan for his comparatively dispassionate approach. He influenced Arai Hakuseki.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hayashi Razan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ideology combined a hierarchical social order, which placed samurai above farmers, artisans and merchants and subordinated women to men and subjects to rulers, with Neo-Confucian political philosophy, promoted by scholars such as <b>Hayashi Razan</b>, which presented benevolent rule by legitimate authority as the basis of social harmony.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1631" aria-label="Kyoto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hayashi Razan"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4013 4510h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4013 4510h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1631">What was here in 1631? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
